        { id: "s18", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  

  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout danger">CLOSING THE ACCOUNTABILITY TRIANGLE: §331 criminalises company management falsifying the accounts; §331a criminalises falsely swearing to their accuracy. §332 is the third piece — criminalising the AUDITOR who should have caught the falsification but instead reports incorrectly, conceals findings, or issues a false opinion. It also continues the PIE-specific aggravation pattern this series has traced through §316a, §323, and §331a.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">The auditor's own criminal exposure — the last piece of the accountability structure</div>
      <div class="principle">§331 (company management falsifying the underlying accounts), §331a (falsely swearing the Bilanzeid), and §332 (the auditor themselves reporting incorrectly or issuing a false opinion) together form a genuinely complete criminal-accountability structure — every party with a hand in producing and verifying the SAME documents this poster series has spent so long documenting faces its own, tailored criminal exposure if they deliberately corrupt the process.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §332 MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Independent verification, given criminal teeth</strong> — the entire audit apparatus this series covered (§§316–324a) exists to give the public confidence in the accounts; §332 protects the INTEGRITY of that verification itself</span>
        <span><strong>PIE-specific escalation, continuing a pattern</strong> — §332(2) Satz 2 and §332(3) apply the SAME heightened scrutiny for public-interest entities already seen in §316a and §323's tiered liability caps</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §332 covers, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core content</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>332(1)</span><span>Base offence — incorrect reporting, concealment, or a false Bestätigungsvermerk (up to 3 years)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>332(2) S.1</span><span>Aggravation for consideration, enrichment, or harm intent (up to 5 years)</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>332(2) S.2</span><span>Separate aggravation for a false opinion at a PIE (up to 5 years)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>332(3)</span><span>A lesser, Leichtfertigkeit-based tier for the PIE-specific case (up to 2 years)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Auditors and Their Assistants</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A Sonderdelikt specifically for the audit function</div>
      <div class="principle">§332 is confirmed to be a Sonderdelikt limited to the Abschlussprüfer (statutory auditor, established across the earlier Audit poster) OR their Gehilfe (assistant) — genuinely different personnel from §331's company management/supervisory board Täterkreis, or §331a's narrower capital-market-oriented legal-representative Täterkreis.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§332(1) — the Base Offence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Three distinct prohibited acts, one shared penalty</div>
      <div class="principle">§332(1) criminalises an auditor or their assistant who: (1) reports INCORRECTLY on the result of the audit of any of the documents listed in Section 4 below; (2) CONCEALS significant circumstances in the Prüfungsbericht (§321, established in the earlier Audit poster); or (3) issues a content-wise INCORRECT Bestätigungsvermerk (§322) — punishable by imprisonment of up to THREE YEARS or a fine.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Documents Covered — Nearly Everything This Series Has Traced</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely comprehensive list, spanning individual, group, and interim statements</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>The Jahresabschluss (individual annual accounts)</span>
        <span>An Einzelabschluss under §325(2a) — see the honest flag in Section 5</span>
        <span>The Lagebericht</span>
        <span>The Konzernabschluss and Konzernlagebericht</span>
        <span>A Zwischenabschluss under §340a(3), or a Konzernzwischenabschluss under §340i(4) — interim statements, primarily relevant to credit-institution-specific provisions this series has not covered in detail</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>An Honest Flag — Conflicting Signals on §325(2a)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Genuine uncertainty worth naming rather than resolving with false confidence</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The earlier §§325–329 poster corrected an EARLIER error by describing §325(2a) as a filing-mechanics provision — "dauerhafte Hinterlegung" (permanent deposit) for large companies' Jahresabschluss. Researching §332 now, the SAME §325(2a) cross-reference appears in a context — "ein Einzelabschluss nach §325 Abs. 2a," listed SEPARATELY from the ordinary Jahresabschluss in the same enumeration — that reads more naturally as referring to a DISTINCT type of individual statement, not merely a filing mechanic applied to the ordinary one. Rather than asserting confident precision in either direction, this poster flags the tension honestly: §325(2a)'s exact substantive character may be more nuanced than either earlier characterisation captured fully, and is worth dedicated verification against the current, complete statutory text if it matters for a specific application.</div>
      <div class="callout warn">This is a deliberate choice to flag uncertainty rather than compound an earlier correction with a new, equally unverified claim.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — a Concealed Going-Concern Risk</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Omitting a known, significant finding from the internal report</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: An auditor who identifies but conceals a genuine going-concern risk</div>
        <div class="wk-body">During an audit, the engagement team identifies clear, significant indicators of going-concern risk — the client is genuinely at risk of insolvency within the coming year — that would ordinarily need to be flagged prominently in the Prüfungsbericht under §321, established in the earlier Audit poster. The lead auditor, aware of this, deliberately OMITS the finding from the report to preserve the client relationship.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The deliberate concealment of this SIGNIFICANT circumstance in the Prüfungsbericht satisfies §332(1)'s second prohibited act, exposing the auditor to imprisonment of up to THREE years or a fine — REGARDLESS of whether the Bestätigungsvermerk itself was otherwise accurate.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§332(2) Sentence 1 — Aggravation for Consideration, Enrichment, or Harm</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A corruption-adjacent aggravating circumstance</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§332(2) sentence 1 raises the maximum penalty to FIVE YEARS' imprisonment (or a fine) where the auditor acts FOR CONSIDERATION (gegen Entgelt) — i.e., in exchange for payment beyond their ordinary audit fee — or with the INTENT to enrich themselves or a third party, or to HARM another party. This is a genuinely corruption-flavoured aggravation, targeting the scenario where a false report or opinion is effectively BOUGHT.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — a Bribed Auditor</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A payment specifically for a false opinion, not merely the ordinary audit fee</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A side payment in exchange for a clean opinion</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An auditor, having identified material misstatements that would ordinarily require an eingeschränkter (qualified) or versagter (adverse) opinion under §322 (established in the earlier Audit poster), instead issues an UNQUALIFIED opinion after receiving a substantial side payment from the client's management, separate from the ordinary engagement fee.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The auditor's conduct satisfies BOTH §332(1)'s base offence (an incorrect Bestätigungsvermerk) AND §332(2) sentence 1's aggravation — having acted "gegen Entgelt" for the side payment — raising the maximum penalty to FIVE years' imprisonment rather than the base three-year maximum.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>§332(2) Sentence 2 — a SEPARATE PIE-Specific Aggravation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The same five-year maximum, triggered by company TYPE rather than corrupt motive</div>
      <div class="principle">§332(2) sentence 2 supplies a GENUINELY SEPARATE aggravating ground, reaching the SAME five-year maximum penalty — this time triggered simply by issuing a content-wise incorrect Bestätigungsvermerk for a public-interest entity (per the §316a definition established in the earlier Audit poster), with NO requirement of bribery, enrichment intent, or any corrupt motive at all. The heightened penalty attaches purely because of the SYSTEMIC significance of a PIE audit failure — continuing the exact same escalation pattern this series has now traced through §316a's own PIE definition and §323's own tiered liability caps.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — a False Opinion on a PIE, No Bribery Required</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Company type alone drives the heightened penalty</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A knowingly false opinion at a listed company, with no financial motive</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An auditor at a listed, capital-market-oriented client (a PIE per §316a) issues an unqualified opinion they KNOW to be false, motivated purely by a desire to avoid a difficult client conversation and preserve the relationship — genuinely no bribery, side payment, or enrichment intent is involved at all.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">DESPITE the absence of any corrupt motive, §332(2) sentence 2 STILL applies purely because the client is a PIE — the maximum penalty remains FIVE years, identical to the bribery scenario in Example 2, since this specific aggravation is triggered by company TYPE rather than by the auditor's underlying motivation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>§332(3) — the Leichtfertigkeit Tier for PIE Cases</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A milder tier, specifically for the PIE scenario when intent cannot be established</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§332(3) supplies a LESSER penalty — imprisonment up to TWO years — specifically for the §332(2) sentence 2 (PIE) scenario where the auditor's conduct amounts only to Leichtfertigkeit (heightened, reckless negligence, the SAME concept established in the earlier §331a poster) rather than genuine intent. This mirrors the SAME degree-of-fault tiering structure this series has now seen repeatedly — most directly parallel to §331a's own three-tier structure covered in the previous poster.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — Contrasting Intent and Leichtfertigkeit for the Same PIE Failure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Identical false opinion, genuinely different consequences depending on the mental state</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: Two auditors, same PIE client, different mental states</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Auditor A at a listed PIE client KNOWS the opinion they're issuing is false and issues it anyway. Auditor B at a different listed PIE client fails to catch a material misstatement due to a genuinely reckless, systematic disregard of clear audit-evidence red flags — falling short of actual knowledge, but well beyond ordinary carelessness.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Auditor A faces up to <strong>FIVE years</strong> under §332(2) sentence 2 — genuine intent regarding a PIE opinion. Auditor B faces up to <strong>TWO years</strong> under §332(3) — Leichtfertigkeit for the SAME category of PIE failure, a genuinely lesser but still criminal consequence, distinct from the merely administrative consequences that would apply if Auditor B's conduct fell all the way down to ordinary negligence instead.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>The Complete Penalty Structure at a Glance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Four genuinely distinct tiers, keyed to both conduct and company type</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-12"><span>Scenario</span><span>Maximum penalty</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-12"><span>Base offence, non-PIE — §332(1)</span><span>3 years or a fine</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-12"><span>Consideration/enrichment/harm intent — §332(2) S.1</span><span>5 years or a fine</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-12"><span>Intentional false opinion on a PIE — §332(2) S.2</span><span>5 years or a fine</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-12"><span>Leichtfertigkeit, PIE case only — §332(3)</span><span>2 years or a fine</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Distinguishing Leichtfertigkeit from ordinary professional error in a specific audit failure — the boundary determining whether §332(3) applies at all</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Proving an auditor's genuine knowledge (rather than mere error) that a specific opinion was false, in a criminal proceeding</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Auditor criminal liability sits entirely outside any accounting standard</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Precisely as with §331 and §331a, IFRS itself has no equivalent — auditor criminal accountability is governed entirely by each jurisdiction's own separate criminal and audit-oversight law, never by an accounting standard itself. §332 completes the same "legal infrastructure beneath any accounting framework" pattern this series has now traced through bookkeeping obligations, retention periods, disclosure filing, and criminal accountability for both preparers and verifiers alike.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §332 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Incorrect Bestätigungsvermerk</span><span><span class="badge bo">Audit opinion</span></span><span>The short, public-facing document established in the earlier Audit poster</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Concealment in the Prüfungsbericht</span><span><span class="badge be">Internal audit report</span></span><span>The detailed, generally non-public report</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §332 connects across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§321, §322</span><span>The Prüfungsbericht and Bestätigungsvermerk §332 directly protects the integrity of</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§316a</span><span>Supplies the PIE definition underlying §332(2) S.2 and §332(3)'s own heightened rules</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§331, §331a</span><span>The two closely related provisions completing the full company-management/Bilanzeid-signer/auditor accountability triangle</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§323</span><span>The CIVIL liability caps established in the earlier Audit poster, operating alongside §332's own separate CRIMINAL exposure for the same underlying conduct</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Gehilfe eines Abschlussprüfers</div><div class="glossary-def">An assistant to the statutory auditor — also within §332's own Täterkreis, alongside the auditor themselves.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Gegen Entgelt</div><div class="glossary-def">"For consideration" — acting in exchange for payment beyond the ordinary audit fee, an aggravating circumstance under §332(2) S.1.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Leichtfertigkeit</div><div class="glossary-def">A heightened, reckless degree of negligence — carrying a lesser but still criminal penalty under §332(3), specifically for PIE cases.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Zwischenabschluss / Konzernzwischenabschluss</div><div class="glossary-def">Interim (individual/group) financial statements, primarily relevant to credit-institution-specific provisions.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bo">Audit opinion</span> The Bestätigungsvermerk
      <span class="badge be">Internal audit report</span> The Prüfungsbericht
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
